The question covers health outcomes for patients and care outcomes. A finding that no study measured STI rates is an evidence gap about one specific health outcome. It does not fall under any out-of-scope entry. STI rates would be a health outcome within scope, since the scope covers health outcomes for patients in general.

Is it material? It is a statement of absence of evidence for a specific outcome. The page presumably addresses health outcomes more broadly. A gap on STI rates is narrow, but it could matter to readers wondering about sexual health. The finding says nothing about effects, only that the outcome was not measured. It is a limited but legitimate note on evidence coverage, and it might be classed as not material because STI is one of many possible outcomes and the question doesn't single it out. However, the review likely listed STI as a pre-specified outcome, so the gap is informative about the limits of the evidence on health outcomes. I lean toward stating it, with caution.

